window.pandaSearch = [{"title": "Overview", "href": "index.html", "text": " \n \n    Python library for molecular simulation \n   From density to contact angle \n   PANDA fits analytical models to one-dimensional density profiles of confined liquids. Estimate a contact angle, identify plausible interface shapes, and compare results across trajectory blocks. \n   Run the quick start \u2192  \n    \n   Synthetic slit-pore roll \u00b7 fitted \u03b8 = 120.61\u00b0  \n \n  Start with your data \n \n  Try a synthetic profile \u2192  Recover a known angle in a slit pore in a few lines of Python.  \n  Analyze a trajectory \u2192  Load simulation frames, compute density, and fit blocks.  \n  Use the command line \u2192  Run the same workflow with shell commands or a TOML file.  \n    What PANDA supports \n The name stands for Predicting Angle from Nanoscale Density Analysis. The library covers slit, triangular, square, and cylindrical pores. The  shape catalog  identifies which models can fit density and which supply surface terms for stability diagrams. \n Explore the related  PANDA Polygon  geometry models and the  PANDA Dynamic  two-angle method.  "}, {"title": "Installation", "href": "installation.html", "text": "The package is named panda-md, the Python module is panda_md, and the shell command is panda.   Install from source \n Use Python 3.10 or newer. This manual targets the developer repository\u2019s 0.1.0 alpha API. The source checkout requires repository access. \n  Shell   git clone https://github.com/Ilya-Garanov-WorkingAcc/PANDA_2.0.git   \n  Shell   cd PANDA_2.0\npython -m venv .venv   \n Activate the environment with  source .venv/bin/activate  on macOS or Linux, or  .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1  in PowerShell. \n  Shell   python -m pip install .   \n   Optional dependencies \n From the same checkout, install the  md  extra for trajectory input and  plot  for Matplotlib figures. \n  Shell   python -m pip install &quot;.[md,plot]&quot;      Check the installation   Shell   python -c &quot;import panda_md; print(panda_md.__version__)&quot;\npanda --help    "}, {"title": "Quick start", "href": "quickstart.html", "text": "Generate a noisy slit-pore roll profile with a known angle, then fit it with PANDA.   Generate and fit a profile \n The slit is 8 nm wide. The synthetic model uses \u03b8 = 120\u00b0, a layer thickness of 0.03 times the slit width, and a liquid volume fraction of 0.40. \n  Python    from  panda_md  import  SlitPore, synthetic_profile\n\npore = SlitPore(width_nm= 8.0 , length_nm= 16.0 )\nroll = pore.interface( &quot;roll&quot; )\ntruth = { &quot;theta&quot; :  120.0 ,  &quot;delta&quot; :  0.03 ,  &quot;phi&quot; :  0.40 }\nprofile = synthetic_profile(\n    roll, truth, density_bulk_per_nm3= 30.0 ,\n    noise_std= 0.005 , seed= 0 ,\n)\nresult = roll.fit(\n    profile, optimize=[ &quot;theta&quot; ,  &quot;delta&quot; ], density_bulk_per_nm3= 30.0 ,\n)\n print ( f&quot;theta = {result.values[&#x27;theta&#x27;]:.2f} deg&quot; )\n print ( f&quot;delta/H = {result.values[&#x27;delta&#x27;]:.4f}&quot; )\n print ( f&quot;phi = {result.values[&#x27;phi&#x27;]:.4f}&quot; )\n print ( f&quot;L1 loss = {result.loss:.5f}&quot; )\n print (result.problems())     Output   theta = 120.61 deg\ndelta/H = 0.0301\nphi = 0.4002\nL1 loss = 0.00377\n[]      Read the result \n These values come from the archived run for this example. PANDA optimizes \u03b8 and \u03b4. It derives \u03c6 from the profile using the supplied bulk density. The last line is the list of fit flags. \n  Download the Python example  \u00b7  Download the normalized profile and fit  \n   Plot the fit and residuals \n With the  plot  extra installed, run this cell after fitting. The residual is the observed normalized density minus the fitted curve at each bin. \n  Python    import  matplotlib.pyplot  as  plt\n from  panda_md.plotting  import  plot_fit\n\nfig, (fit_ax, residual_ax) = plt.subplots(\n     2 ,  1 , sharex= True , height_ratios=( 2 ,  1 ), layout= &quot;constrained&quot; \n)\nplot_fit(result, ax=fit_ax)\nfit_ax.set_xlabel( &quot;&quot; )\nresidual_ax.axhline( 0 , color= &quot;0.5&quot; , linewidth= 1 )\nresidual_ax.plot(result.coordinate, result.data - result.model_curve,  &quot;.&quot; , markersize= 3 )\nresidual_ax. set (xlabel= &quot;Reduced coordinate, z/H&quot; , ylabel= &quot;Data \u2212 fit&quot; )\nfig.savefig( &quot;roll-fit.png&quot; , dpi= 150 )\nplt.show()      Plot from the archived synthetic roll fit.  Download PNG .     Use your own data \n Follow the  trajectory guide  for simulation output. If you already have a density profile, see  fitting a saved profile .  "}, {"title": "Pores and shapes", "href": "geometries.html", "text": "A pore defines the confinement geometry. A named shape defines the analytical interface inside it.   Set up a pore \n     Geometry  Python class  Characteristic size    \n  Slit   SlitPore   Wall-to-wall width H  \n  Triangle   TrianglePore   Side length a  \n  Square   SquarePore   Side length a  \n  Cylinder   CylinderPore   Tube diameter D  \n   \n  pore.interface(\"roll\")  selects an analytical model for the slit. Polygon names encode the number of walls touched ( w ) and corners occupied ( c ). \n   Shape catalog \n This snapshot lists 26 shapes. Of these, 18 supply a density profile and 24 supply surface terms. Availability describes the implemented formulas. Check the individual angle and volume-fraction limits before fitting. \n \n Pore   All pores  Slit  Triangle  Square  Cylinder   \n Operation   All operations  Density profile  Surface terms   \n   \n     Pore  Shape  Density profile  Surface terms      Slit   droplet   Yes  Yes    Slit   roll   Yes  Yes    Slit   worm   Yes  Yes    Slit   layer   Yes  Yes    Slit   doughnut   Yes  \u2014    Slit   perforation   Yes  \u2014    Triangle   capsule_w3c0   Yes  Yes    Triangle   droplet_w1c0   Yes  Yes    Triangle   filament_w0c0   Yes  Yes    Triangle   filament_w3c0   Yes  Yes    Triangle   droplet_w2c0   \u2014  Yes    Triangle   droplet_w3c0   \u2014  Yes    Triangle   filament_w0c0_straight   \u2014  Yes    Triangle   filament_w1c0   \u2014  Yes    Triangle   filament_w2c0   \u2014  Yes    Triangle   filament_w2c1   \u2014  Yes    Triangle   filament_w3c1   \u2014  Yes    Triangle   filament_w3c2   \u2014  Yes    Square   capsule_w4c0   Yes  Yes    Square   droplet_w1c0   Yes  Yes    Square   droplet_w2c0   Yes  Yes    Square   droplet_w2c1   Yes  Yes    Square   filament_w0c0   Yes  Yes    Square   filament_w0c0_straight   Yes  Yes    Square   filament_w1c0   Yes  Yes    Cylinder   capsule   Yes  Yes     \n Run  panda shapes --pore triangle  to see the installed version\u2019s models, parameter bounds, and regimes. \n   Limits of the models \n Slit and cylindrical contact-angle models cover the non-wetting side of 90\u00b0. Some polygon models have narrower domains. Models such as a flat  layer  have no contact-angle parameter. Check  model.is_feasible(...)  and  model.phi_limits(...)  for the specific shape.    Fit a triangular filament \n This example fits a synthetic  filament_w3c0  profile. The filament touches three walls and occupies zero corners. \n  Python    from  panda_md  import  TrianglePore, synthetic_profile\n\npore = TrianglePore(side_nm= 6 , length_nm= 12 , layer_width_nm= 6 )\nfilament = pore.interface( &quot;filament_w3c0&quot; )\ntruth = { &quot;theta&quot; :  150.0 ,  &quot;delta&quot; :  0.02 ,  &quot;phi&quot; :  0.5 }\nprofile = synthetic_profile(\n    filament, truth, density_bulk_per_nm3= 30 , noise_std= 0.005 , seed= 0 \n)\nresult = filament.fit(profile, density_bulk_per_nm3= 30 )\n print ( f&quot;Input theta = {truth[&#x27;theta&#x27;]:.2f} deg&quot; )\n print ( f&quot;Fit theta = {result.values[&#x27;theta&#x27;]:.2f} deg&quot; )\n print ( f&quot;Flags: {result.problems()}&quot; )     Output   Input theta = 150.00 deg\nFit theta = 149.83 deg\nFlags: []    With the  plot  extra, draw the fitted curve over the synthetic data.   Python    import  matplotlib.pyplot  as  plt\n from  panda_md.plotting  import  plot_fit\n\nax = plot_fit(result)\nax.set_xlabel( &quot;Reduced coordinate, z/a&quot; )\nax.figure.savefig( &quot;triangle-fit.png&quot; , dpi= 150 , bbox_inches= &quot;tight&quot; )\nplt.show()      Archived synthetic fit for a triangular pore.  Download CSV  \u00b7  Download PNG .   "}, {"title": "From a trajectory", "href": "trajectories.html", "text": "Build a density profile from molecular-dynamics frames, then fit the average and compare time blocks.   Load the simulation \n Install the  md  extra. The example uses GROMACS files. MDAnalysis also supports formats such as LAMMPS. Replace the topology, trajectory, selections, equilibration cutoff, and bulk density with values for your simulation. \n  Python    import  panda_md  as  panda\n\ntraj = panda.load_trajectory( &quot;system.tpr&quot; ,  &quot;system.xtc&quot; )\npore = panda.SlitPore.from_trajectory(traj, walls= &quot;resname GRAPHITE&quot; )\nprofiles = panda.density_profile(\n    traj, pore,  &quot;resname WATER and name OW&quot; ,\n    start= &quot;2 ns&quot; , block= &quot;1 ns&quot; , bins= 200 , centering= &quot;pore&quot; ,\n)\nprofiles.save( &quot;water.npz&quot; )      Fit and compare time blocks   Python   roll = pore.interface( &quot;roll&quot; )\nspec = panda.FitSpec(density_bulk_per_nm3= 33.1 )\nresult = roll.fit(profiles.mean(), density_bulk_per_nm3= 33.1 )\nblocks = panda.fit_blocks(roll, profiles, spec)\n print (result.summary())\n print (blocks.std( &quot;theta&quot; ))   \n The fit of the mean profile and the mean of the individual block fits are distinct estimates. Choose blocks long enough to reduce correlation and inspect per-block flags before interpreting the spread or standard error. \n   Plot trajectory diagnostics \n With the  plot  extra, plot the fitted angle at each block midpoint and inspect the two-dimensional density map. Both plots depend on the trajectory and selections above. \n  Python    import  matplotlib.pyplot  as  plt\n from  panda_md.plotting  import  plot_blocks, plot_density_map\n\nplot_blocks(blocks, parameter= &quot;theta&quot; )\ndensity = panda.density_map(\n    traj, pore,  &quot;resname WATER and name OW&quot; , shape= &quot;roll&quot; , start= &quot;2 ns&quot; \n)\nplot_density_map(density)\nplt.show()      Check the profile \n Match the bulk density to the selected particle representation. Check wall selections, centering, bin width, and the periodic box geometry. Use  density_map(..., shape=\"roll\")  to inspect the two-dimensional morphology if multiple models fit similarly. \n For saved results use  ProfileSet.load(\"water.npz\") . For a single profile use  DensityProfile.load(...) .  "}, {"title": "Fitting and interpretation", "href": "fitting.html", "text": "Fit a candidate interface, inspect its diagnostics, and compare models in the physically accessible domain.   Fit a saved profile \n A headerless CSV contains coordinate in nm, number density in nm\u207b\u00b3, and an optional uncertainty column. Center the coordinates consistently with the analytical pore. The normalized CSV in the quick start is for plotting. \n  Python    from  panda_md  import  DensityProfile, SlitPore\n\nprofile = DensityProfile.load( &quot;density.csv&quot; )\npore = SlitPore(width_nm= 8.0 , length_nm= 16.0 )\nresult = pore.interface( &quot;roll&quot; ).fit(\n    profile, optimize=[ &quot;theta&quot; ,  &quot;delta&quot; ],\n    bounds={ &quot;theta&quot; : ( 95.0 ,  175.0 )}, density_bulk_per_nm3= 33.1 ,\n)\n print (result.summary())\n print (result.problems())      Parameters and normalization \n  theta  is the contact angle in degrees for the analyzed phase.  delta  is the effective wall-layer thickness as a fraction of the characteristic pore size.  phi  is the analyzed liquid\u2019s volume fraction.  shift  is an offset along the profile coordinate in pore-size units, fixed at zero by default. \n With bulk normalization, PANDA divides the input density by \u03c1 bulk  and can estimate \u03c6 from the profile. With mean normalization, supply the profile\u2019s particle count and pore volume, and fix or optimize \u03c6 if the bulk density is unavailable. \n   Fit diagnostics \n The default L1 objective is the mean absolute deviation of the normalized profiles. PANDA runs Nelder\u2013Mead from multiple starting points. Inspect convergence, feasibility, parameter bounds, and the  at_regime_boundary  flag. A boundary fit near 90\u00b0 may indicate that the selected model\u2019s angular range is too restrictive. \n   Compare shapes and stability \n  compare_shapes(pore, profile, spec)  ranks available profiles by fit loss. Near-ties can occur when different interfaces project to nearly the same one-dimensional density. Inspect the density map or snapshots before assigning a morphology. \n  stability_diagram(...)  ranks feasible shapes by the capillary surface term S LV  \u2212 cos \u03b8 S SL  at prescribed angle and layer thickness. Each operation has its own set of supported shapes in the  catalog .  "}, {"title": "Command line", "href": "cli.html", "text": "Use panda to build profiles, fit a known shape, or compare candidate models from the shell.   Inspect available models   Shell   panda shapes --pore slit\npanda profile --help\npanda fit --help      Create a profile and fit it   Shell   panda profile system.tpr system.xtc --walls &quot;resname GRAPHITE&quot; --select &quot;resname WATER and name OW&quot; --start &quot;2 ns&quot; --block &quot;1 ns&quot; -o water.npz --pore-output pore.json\npanda fit water.npz --pore-file pore.json --shape roll --rho-bulk 33.1 --blocks -o result.json   \n Use  --shape auto  to compare supported candidates and  --plot fit.png  with the plotting extra. The exit codes are 0 for success, 1 for an input error, and 2 for a completed fit with diagnostic flags. \n   Save the workflow in TOML   TOML   [pore]\ntype = &quot;slit&quot;\nwall_selection = &quot;resname GRAPHITE&quot;\n\n[profile]\ntopology = &quot;system.tpr&quot;\ntrajectory = [&quot;system.xtc&quot;]\nselect = &quot;resname WATER and name OW&quot;\nstart = &quot;2 ns&quot;\nblock = &quot;1 ns&quot;\n\n[fit]\nshape = &quot;roll&quot;\ndensity_bulk_per_nm3 = 33.1\nblocks = true\noutput = &quot;result.json&quot;    Save as  panda.toml  and run  panda run panda.toml . Input paths are relative to the settings file.  "}, {"title": "Python API", "href": "api.html", "text": "A reference to the main objects and functions in the panda_md namespace.   Geometry and fitting \n   SlitPore ,  TrianglePore ,  SquarePore ,  CylinderPore   Store pore dimensions in nm. Call  pore.interface(shape)  to construct an  InterfaceModel . \n  InterfaceModel.fit(profile, **settings)   Fit one morphology to one  DensityProfile . \n  FitSpec   Reusable fit settings, including normalization, free and fixed parameters, and bounds. \n  FitResult   Inspect  values ,  loss ,  summary() , and  problems() .  \n   Profiles and trajectories \n   load_trajectory(topology, *trajectory_files, **options)   Read MD files through MDAnalysis. For in-memory data use  ArrayTrajectory . \n  density_profile(trajectory, pore, select, ...)   Build density profiles, optionally split into time blocks. \n  DensityProfile ,  ProfileSet   Represent one profile or a series. A profile set provides  mean() ,  sem() , and  block(i) . \n  density_map(...) ,  synthetic_profile(...)   Compute a two-dimensional density map or generate a synthetic one-dimensional profile from known model parameters.  \n   Analysis and plotting \n   fit_blocks(model, profiles, spec=None)   Fit each block and summarize parameter means and spread. \n  compare_shapes(pore, profile, spec)   Rank fitted models and report skipped or near-indistinguishable candidates. \n  stability_diagram(pore, *, contact_angle_deg, wall_layer, volume_fractions, length_ratios, shape_names=None)   Find minimum-energy candidates over a grid of liquid fractions and reduced pore lengths. \n  panda_md.plotting   Optional plotting helpers including  plot_fit  and  plot_density_map .  \n For full signatures and extension guides, see the  developer manual  (repository access required).  "}, {"title": "Citing PANDA", "href": "citation.html", "text": "Cite the original PANDA method and the studies relevant to your geometry or analysis.   Original method \n Semenchuk, Kondratyuk, and Kopanichuk introduced PANDA in  Colloids and Surfaces A , volume 708, article 135994. Include the package version or commit used in your methods. \n  BibTeX   @article{semenchuk2025panda,\n  author  = {Semenchuk, A. A. and Kondratyuk, N. D. and Kopanichuk, I. V.},\n  title   = {{PANDA}: Predicting angle from nanoscale density analysis},\n  journal = {Colloids and Surfaces A: Physicochemical and Engineering Aspects},\n  year    = {2025},\n  volume  = {708},\n  pages   = {135994},\n  doi     = {10.1016/j.colsurfa.2024.135994}\n}     Download BibTeX     Package paper  The manuscript describing the current software package is in preparation. Its citation will be added when it is available.    Related publications    The shape of the liquid-liquid interface for oil/water mixtures in slit pores  Kopanichuk et al. \u00b7 Colloids and Surfaces A 601 (2020), 124884    PANDA: Predicting angle from nanoscale density analysis  Semenchuk, Kondratyuk, and Kopanichuk \u00b7 Colloids and Surfaces A 708 (2025), 135994    Towards reproducible wetting studies: Automated contact angle determination by molecular simulations  Semenchuk et al. \u00b7 Colloids and Surfaces A 725 (2025), 137586    Characterisation of the dominant liquid-liquid interface shape in confined systems during molecular simulations  Shistko et al. \u00b7 Molecular Simulation 51(16) (2025), 1071\u20131080    From nanodroplets to macroscopic wettability for rough surfaces: Combining molecular simulations and experiments  Guskov et al. \u00b7 Journal of Molecular Liquids 439 (2025), 128861    The Effect of Adsorption Film Thickness on the Shape of the Interface Between Immiscible Liquids in a Flat Slitlike Pore  Semenchuk, Bakulin, Gerke, Kondratyuk, and Kopanichuk \u00b7 Colloid Journal 88(2) (2026), 211\u2013221    Assessing density equilibration in confined liquid\u2013liquid interfaces  Guskov et al. \u00b7 Molecular Simulation 52(7) (2026), 546\u2013555    The interface shape and contact angle determination for liquid-liquid systems in cylindrical nanopores  Vandyshev, Kondratyuk, Gerke, and Kopanichuk \u00b7 Capillarity 19(3) (2026), 86\u201396      Dynamic contact angles   PANDA-D: Interface-free determination of two dynamic contact angles  (preprint, 2026). The two-angle extension jointly analyzes density and layerwise center-of-mass displacement.  "}];
